\section{Modularity}{


\strong{\code{ramp.xds}} is designed for modular computation. The
mathematical framework has
five core biological processes organized
into three chunks called \strong{dynamical components} (see \link{xds_info_dynamical_components}).
The modular design
makes it possible to study a system that with all three components fully defined (using \link{xds_setup}), or to
develop systems of equations that isolate and study some part
of a system.

To implement \emph{plug-and-play} modularity, a trivial module was developed
for each component. The trivial model ignores inputs from upstream modules, and it congfigures a
a \emph{trace function} to pass outputs downstream (see \link{xds_info_trivial_forcing}).
}

\section{Basic Setup Functions}{


Depending on the purpose of the study,
\strong{Basic Setup} is done by one of five functions:
\itemize{
\item \link{xds_setup} can be used for most studies. It configures all three dynamical components.
\item \link{xds_setup_human} sets up an \strong{XH} module to study disease dynamics forced by infective biting in the patches.
\item \link{xds_setup_eir} sets up an \strong{XH} module to study disease dynamics forced by the daily EIR. Use this to study cohort dynamics using \link{xds_solve_cohort}.
\item \link{xds_setup_mosy} sets a model for mosquito ecology that ignores infection dynamics.
\item \link{xds_setup_aquatic} sets up a model for aquatic dynamics forced by egg laying.
}

Each function sets \code{class(xds_obj$frame)} to dispatch functions that are used to solve dynamical systems.
}

\section{\link{xds_setup}}{


\link{xds_setup} sets \code{class(xds_obj$frame)="full"} and requires arguments to set up all three dynamical components.
