\section{\link{xds_setup_mosy}}{


\link{xds_setup_mosy} sets \code{class(xds_obj$frame)="mosy"} for models of mosquito ecology.
It
\itemize{
\item sets up a \strong{L} module
\item sets up a \strong{MY} module, but the \strong{Y} processes are ignored
\item sets up a trivial \strong{XH} module to pass human population density
}
}

\section{\link{xds_setup_aquatic}}{


\link{xds_setup_aquatic} sets \code{class(xds_obj$frame)="aquatic"}for models of aquatic mosquito ecology. It
\itemize{
\item sets up a \strong{L} module
\item sets up a trivial \strong{MY} module and configures a trace function for egg laying
\item sets up an \strong{XH} component to be ignored
}
}

\section{\link{xds_setup_human}}{


\link{xds_setup_human} sets \code{class(xds_obj$frame)="human"} for models of malaria epidemiology or other disease dynamics forced by infective biting in the patches. It
\itemize{
\item sets up the \strong{XH} module
\item sets up a trivial \strong{MY} component and configures a trace function for infective biting in patches
\item sets up an \strong{L} component to be ignored
}
}

\section{\link{xds_setup_eir}}{


\link{xds_setup_eir} sets \code{class(xds_obj$frame)="eir"} for models of malaria epidemiology or other disease dynamics forced by the EIR. These models
can also be solved by \link{xds_solve} or \link{xds_solve_cohort}: in the former, \strong{time} is the independent variable, and in the latter \strong{age.}
Unlike the other variants, these models
lack the \strong{MY} and \strong{L} components. Instead, it
\itemize{
\item sets up the \strong{XH} module
\itemize{
\item the average eir
\item a seasonal pattern function
\item a trend pattern function
